From: Igni Li <igni.li@quectel.com>
Subject: [PATCH] vidc: stop streaming and release the queues when a session is closed

A client that goes away while its session is streaming leaves the session
behind, and that is the ordinary case rather than only a crash: closing the
player's window does it too, because the player exits without tearing the
stream down.  Only letting the clip play to its end (EOS) is clean.

That session stays in core->instances in the CLOSE (4) state and is still
charged for the load it used to run, so on QCM6490 (a 4K frame is 240 x 135 =
32400 macroblocks and MAX_MBPF is 77522, i.e. room for two of them) two such
events reject every later 4K session until the VPU is rebound or the board is
rebooted:

    msm_vidc: 7461afc0: avcD_0: msm_vidc_check_core_mbpf:
      video overloaded. needed 97200, max 77522
    msm_vidc: 7461afc0: avcD_0: msm_vidc_check_session_supported:
      current session not supported
    msm_vidc: 7461afc0: avcD_0: msm_vidc_streamon: vb2_streamon(10) failed, -12
    msm_vidc: 7461afc0: avcD_0: msm_v4l2_reqbufs: inst in error state

which is what makes 4K playback fall back to software decoding.

Measured on QCM6490, clean boot, hwdec=vaapi, 4K H.264, sampled while the clip
was still playing so that the verdict is the client's own report:

    leaked sessions   decoder instances   mpv reported
    0                 1                   Using hardware decoding (vaapi)
    1                 2                   Using hardware decoding (vaapi)
    2                 3                   none - fell back to software:
                                          "Failed to create decode context:
                                           2 (resource allocation failed)"

Retrying at +15/+30/+60/+120 s was rejected every time.

Simply not counting CLOSE/ERROR sessions in the load checks is not an option:
those sessions do hold firmware heap, the leak is real.  Skipping them in
msm_vidc_check_core_mbpf() lets the next session be started on top of a
firmware that is genuinely out of memory, and the board hard resets.  Measured
with a patch that ignored dead sessions in the load accounting, 4K H.264,
killed midway once per round to leak one more session each time:

    leaked sessions   result
    0..4              Using hardware decoding (vaapi), no streamon failures
    5                 board hard reset while playing

So the leak has to be prevented, not discounted.  Two independent defects on
the close path cause it.

1) The firmware session is closed while the queues are still streaming.
   msm_vidc_close() sends HFI_CMD_SESSION_CLOSE with both queues possibly
   streaming; they are only stopped later, by the queue teardown in
   msm_vidc_close_helper(), which runs after the firmware session is already
   gone, and the firmware then reports:

       VenusHostDriver_ParseC2Command(1229): Memory leak found:
       current heap status: 126cd, expected heap status : 0

   A client that does streamoff before close never hits this, which is why a
   clip that plays to its end is clean and why an exiting player is not.  Stop
   the queues first: output (frames) then input (bitstream), the same order the
   queue teardown uses.  A client that already stopped them pays nothing, since
   vb2_is_streaming() is false.

2) The queues are released only once the instance reference count has reached
   zero, but a buffer holds a reference on the instance: msm_vb2_alloc() takes
   it and msm_vb2_put() drops it, and msm_vb2_put() runs when the queue is
   released.  A client that exits without VIDIOC_REQBUFS(0) - killed while
   playing - therefore keeps its instance alive forever: it stays in
   core->instances in the CLOSE state, still charged for the load it used to
   run, and every later 4K session is rejected.  Release the queues before
   dropping the last reference; msm_vidc_vb2_queue_deinit() is idempotent (it
   returns early once m2m_dev is NULL), so the call that close_helper() makes
   afterwards turns into a no-op.

Both changes are needed: 1) on its own still leaves the instance behind and 2)
on its own still leaks the firmware heap.

With both, closing or killing the player in the middle of playback leaves no
session behind, so 4K playback keeps using the hardware decoder and no longer
needs the VPU to be rebound or the board to be rebooted.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Qualcomm BSP specific]

Signed-off-by: Igni Li <igni.li@quectel.com>
---
 vidc/src/msm_vidc.c | 55 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 55 insertions(+)
--- a/vidc/src/msm_vidc.c
+++ b/vidc/src/msm_vidc.c
@@ -921,6 +921,43 @@ fail_add_session:
 	return NULL;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Stop the queues of a session that is about to be closed, while the firmware
+ * session still exists.
+ *
+ * A client that goes away without an explicit streamoff - mpv being closed or
+ * killed while it is playing, for instance - leaves the session with its ports
+ * still streaming.  The firmware cannot free such a session cleanly and reports
+ * "Memory leak found: current heap status: ..., expected heap status : 0"; that
+ * memory is never reclaimed, and the leaked instance still counts as a live 4K
+ * session, so after two of them every further 4K session is refused with
+ * -ENOMEM until the VPU is rebound or the board is rebooted - which is what
+ * makes 4K playback silently fall back to software decoding.  Stopping the
+ * queues first lets the firmware close the session properly.
+ *
+ * The queue teardown in msm_vidc_close_helper() does stop the queues as well,
+ * but only after msm_vidc_session_close() has already killed the firmware
+ * session, which is too late.  Output (frames) first, then input (bitstream):
+ * the same order the queue teardown uses.
+ */
+static void msm_vidc_stop_streaming_before_close(struct msm_vidc_inst *inst)
+{
+	enum msm_vidc_port_type ports[2] = { OUTPUT_PORT, INPUT_PORT };
+	struct vb2_queue *q;
+	int i, rc;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
+		q = inst->bufq[ports[i]].vb2q;
+		if (!q || !vb2_is_streaming(q))
+			continue;
+
+		rc = vb2_streamoff(q, q->type);
+		if (rc)
+			i_vpr_e(inst, "%s: streamoff(%d) failed: %d\n",
+				__func__, q->type, rc);
+	}
+}
+
 int msm_vidc_close(struct msm_vidc_inst *inst)
 {
 	int rc = 0;
@@ -936,6 +973,7 @@ int msm_vidc_close(struct msm_vidc_inst
 	/* print internal buffer memory usage stats */
 	msm_vidc_print_memory_stats(inst);
 	msm_vidc_print_residency_stats(core);
+	msm_vidc_stop_streaming_before_close(inst);
 	msm_vidc_session_close(inst);
 	msm_vidc_change_state(inst, MSM_VIDC_CLOSE, __func__);
 	inst->sub_state = MSM_VIDC_SUB_STATE_NONE;
@@ -945,6 +983,26 @@ int msm_vidc_close(struct msm_vidc_inst
 	cancel_stability_work_sync(inst);
 	cancel_stats_work_sync(inst);
 	msm_vidc_show_stats(inst);
+	/*
+	 * Release the queues before dropping the instance reference.
+	 *
+	 * A buffer allocated through msm_vb2_alloc() takes a reference on the
+	 * instance and drops it again from msm_vb2_put(), which runs when the
+	 * queue is released.  The queue used to be released only from
+	 * msm_vidc_close_helper(), i.e. after the reference count had already
+	 * reached zero, so a client that exits without VIDIOC_REQBUFS(0) - killed
+	 * while it was playing - kept its instance alive forever: the instance
+	 * stayed in core->instances in the CLOSE state and went on being charged
+	 * for the load it used to run.  Two such instances are enough to make
+	 * every further 4K session fail with -ENOMEM on QCM6490 until the VPU is
+	 * rebound or the board is rebooted.  Release the queues here instead;
+	 * msm_vidc_vb2_queue_deinit() is idempotent, so the call close_helper()
+	 * makes afterwards turns into a no-op, and for a client that did tear its
+	 * queues down the queues are already released and this costs nothing.
+	 */
+	inst_lock(inst, __func__);
+	msm_vidc_vb2_queue_deinit(inst);
+	inst_unlock(inst, __func__);
 	put_inst(inst);
 	msm_vidc_schedule_core_deinit(core);
 
